threadpool: honour LWS_TP_STATUS_STOPPING in task_function

From: Andy Green <andy@warmcat.com>

task_function() ignored its status argument entirely.  The lws threadpool
contract (see lib/misc/threadpool/README.md) is that a task is only ever
told LWS_TP_STATUS_RUNNING or LWS_TP_STATUS_STOPPING, and STOPPING means
"clean up and return LWS_TP_RETURN_STOPPED".

For blame, a single jg2_ctx_fill() does the whole git_blame_file() plus
all the per-hunk git_commit_lookup() work synchronously before returning
(lib/job/blame.c, job_blame_lines()), with no check-in in between.  So a
STOPPING request arriving because the bound wsi closed (crawler dropped
the connection, or lws_threadpool_dequeue_task() asked us to stop) was
invisible until the entire expensive call returned.

Worse, once such a task finishes its fill it returns LWS_TP_RETURN_SYNC
and the worker parks in lws_threadpool_worker_sync() waiting on a
service-thread callback for a wsi that is already gone.  The worker then
sits on its pool slot, looping through SYNC timeouts, for up to the
~5 minute (100 x 3s) safety window.  Under a crawl, four blame tasks in
this state pin all four pool slots and the pool deadlocks.

Check the status at entry and bail out with STOPPED immediately when
asked to stop, before any jg2_ctx_fill() work.  This collapses the
lingering-in-SYNC path to a single cycle and lets the pool drain when
connections go away.
---
 src/protocol_gitohashi.c |   17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/protocol_gitohashi.c b/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
index 2059e98..6a6519b 100644
--- a/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
+++ b/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
@@ -78,6 +78,23 @@ task_function(void *user, enum lws_threadpool_task_status s)
 	int n, flags = 0, opa;
 	char outlive = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * If the wsi we are bound to went away (client closed the connection,
+	 * or the request was dequeued), the threadpool asks us to stop by
+	 * passing LWS_TP_STATUS_STOPPING.  We must honour it promptly and
+	 * return STOPPED, otherwise this worker stays pinned until the
+	 * (potentially very long) jg2_ctx_fill / git blame completes, and
+	 * worse, once we return SYNC the worker parks in the sync wait
+	 * dependent on a service thread that may never come for a wsi that
+	 * is already gone.
+	 *
+	 * See the lws threadpool README: a task is only ever told RUNNING or
+	 * STOPPING in the status argument, and STOPPING means clean up and
+	 * return LWS_TP_RETURN_STOPPED.
+	 */
+	if (s == LWS_TP_STATUS_STOPPING)
+		return LWS_TP_RETURN_STOPPED;
+
 	/*
 	 * first time, we must do the http reply, and either acquire the
 	 * jg2 ctx or finish the transaction
